@page "/visualmcp"
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "VisualMCP";
    ViewData["Description"] = "VisualMCP — a local MCP server that gives AI coding agents the IDE's brain for C#/.NET: Roslyn semantics, compiler-gated refactorings, debugging, profiling and decompilation as tools.";
}

<header class="page-head project-head">
    <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Project</p>
        <h1>VisualMCP</h1>
        <p class="lede">
            A local MCP server that gives AI coding agents the IDE's brain for
            C#/.NET. More than 400 tools built on Roslyn, MSBuild and a real
            debugger, so the agent asks the compiler instead of grepping text.
        </p>
    </div>
</header>

<p class="project-intro">
    “Your agent is fluent in JavaScript. It guesses at C#.” VisualMCP exists so
    that it stops guessing: every question about the code goes through the same
    semantic model an IDE uses, and every edit is checked against the compiler
    before it touches the disk. The code and the docs are on
    <a href="https://github.com/matteofabbri/VisualMCP" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>.
</p>

<ol class="feature-list">
    <li class="feature">
        <span class="feature-number" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
        <div class="feature-body">
            <h2>It reads code the way the compiler does</h2>
            <p class="feature-role">Semantic model, not regex</p>
            <p>
                Symbols, references, callers and implementations are resolved
                through Roslyn, so overloads, generics, partial types and
                interface dispatch are answered exactly — the way Visual
                Studio would answer them, not the way a text search would.
            </p>
        </div>
    </li>

    <li class="feature">
        <span class="feature-number" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
        <div class="feature-body">
            <h2>Refactorings that refuse to break the build</h2>
            <p class="feature-role">Compiler-gated edits</p>
            <p>
                Rename, extract, move and change-signature operations are
                verified against the compiler before anything is written. An
                edit that would introduce an error is refused, and every write
                is recorded so it can be reverted.
            </p>
        </div>
    </li>

    <li class="feature">
        <span class="feature-number" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
        <div class="feature-body">
            <h2>The whole dev loop, in the conversation</h2>
            <p class="feature-role">Build, test, debug, profile</p>
            <p>
                Build the solution, run the tests with coverage, attach a
                debugger with breakpoints, run benchmarks, profile CPU,
                allocations, GC and lock contention — all without the agent
                ever leaving your repo's context.
            </p>
        </div>
    </li>

    <li class="feature">
        <span class="feature-number" aria-hidden="true">04</span>
        <div class="feature-body">
            <h2>It reads binaries too</h2>
            <p class="feature-role">ILSpy and Ghidra</p>
            <p>
                .NET assemblies are decompiled with ILSpy and native binaries
                recovered with Ghidra; the result lands in the same queryable
                model as the source code, so “what does this library actually
                do?” has an answer.
            </p>
        </div>
    </li>

    <li class="feature">
        <span class="feature-number" aria-hidden="true">05</span>
        <div class="feature-body">
            <h2>Local first</h2>
            <p class="feature-role">No account, no hosted service</p>
            <p>
                One local process, talking to your MCP client — Claude Code,
                ZCode and friends. Nothing about your code leaves the machine.
            </p>
        </div>
    </li>
</ol>

